<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-limits-indeterminate" >
<title>Inderminate Forms in Limits</title>

<introduction>

<p>Standards
    <ul>
        <li>Evaluate a limit requiring L'Hôpital's Rule</li>
        <li>Evaluate a limit involving exponential indeterminate form</li>
    </ul>
</p>

<p>While we can prove limit properties for sums of functions both of which are infinite or products of functions both of which are infinite, we have avoided forms such as <m>\infty-\infty</m> and <m>0/0</m>. In this section we will illustrate the reason these are a problem and learn ways to handle these forms when they occur in limits.</p>

</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-indeterminate-discover">
    <title>Variability of Indeterminate Forms</title>

    <p>Use the illustrations in <xref ref="interactive-indeterminate-example1" /> and <xref ref="interactive-indeterminate-example2" /> to see why limits of the form <m>0/0</m> and <m>1^\infty</m> cannot be evaluated directly.</p>

    <figure xml:id="interactive-indeterminate-example1">
        <caption>Illustration of Limit Form 0/0</caption>
        <interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="4:3" preview="images/indeterminate_ex1.png" label="interactive-indeterminate-example1">
            <slate xml:id="geogebra-indeterminate-example1" surface="geogebra" material="vagdvcnh" aspect="4:3">
                    setCoordSystem(-1, 3, 0, 6);
                    enableShiftDragZoom(true);
            </slate>
            <instructions>
                <p>Change the parameter <m>a</m> in the function <m>f(x)=\frac{e^{ax}-1}{x}</m> to see the value <m>\lim_{x \to 0} f(x)</m>.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <activity xml:id="activity-indeterminate-all-values1">
        <title>Why 0/0 is not determined</title>
        <introduction><p>Using the interactive illustration above we will illustrate that the expression <m>0/0</m> in a limit can equal any real value. This will demonstrate that we cannot write the answer to a limit evaluation when we see <m>0/0</m>.</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Evaluate <m>\frac{e^{1 \cdot x}-1}{x}</m> at <m>x=0</m>. Write down the expression you obtain.</p></task>
        <task><p>Make sure the slider is at <m>a=1</m>. Based on the graph what does it appear the function value is at <m>x=0</m>? Note this would be the limit value.</p></task>
        <task><p>Evaluate <m>\frac{e^{2 \cdot x}-1}{x}</m> at <m>x=0</m>. Write down the expression you obtain.</p></task>
        <task><p>Move the slider to <m>a=2</m>. Based on the graph what does it appear the function value is at <m>x=0</m>? Note this would be the limit value.</p></task>
        <task><p>Move the slider around. For <m>a</m> between 1 and 5 what values does the function appear to take on at <m>x=0</m>?</p></task>
        <task><p>Based on this experiment why does the expression <m>0/0</m> not determine a specific answer?</p></task>
    </activity>

    <figure xml:id="interactive-indeterminate-example2">
        <caption>Illustration of Limit Form <m>1^\infty</m></caption>
        <interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="4:3" preview="images/indeterminate_ex2.png" label="interactive-indeterminate-example2">
            <slate xml:id="geogebra-indeterminate-example2" surface="geogebra" material="t9he83te" aspect="4:3">
                    setCoordSystem(-2, 3, 0, 6);
                    enableShiftDragZoom(true);
            </slate>
            <instructions>
                <p>Change the parameter <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> in the function <m>f(x)=(1+ax)^{b/x}</m> to see the value <m>\lim_{x \to 0^+} f(x)</m>.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <activity xml:id="activity-indeterminate-all-values2">
        <title>Why <m>1^\infty</m> is not determined</title>
        <introduction><p>Using the interactive illustration above we will illustrate that the expression <m>1^\infty</m> in a limit can equal any real value. This will demonstrate that we cannot write the answer to a limit evaluation when we see <m>1^\infty</m>.</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Evaluate <m>(1+1x)^{1/x}</m> at <m>x=0</m>. Write down the expression you obtain.</p></task>
        <task><p>Make sure the sliders are at <m>a=1</m> and <m>b=1</m>. Based on the graph what does it appear the function value is at <m>x=0</m>? Note this would be the limit value.</p></task>
        <task><p>Evaluate <m>(1+2x)^{1/x}</m> at <m>x=0</m>. Write down the expression you obtain.</p></task>
        <task><p>Move the slider to <m>a=2</m>. Based on the graph what does it appear the function value is at <m>x=0</m>? Note this would be the limit value.</p></task>
        <task><p>Move the slider around. For <m>a</m> between 1 and 3 what values does the function appear to take on at <m>x=0</m>?</p></task>
        <task><p>Based on this experiment why does the expression <m>1^\infty</m> not determine a specific answer?</p></task>
    </activity>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection-indeterminate-lhopital">
        <title>Indeterminate Forms <m>0/0</m> and <m>\infty/\infty</m></title>

        <table xml:id="table-indeterminate-forms">
            <title>Indeterminate Forms</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="15%" />
                <row><cell><p><m>\frac{0}{0}</m></p></cell></row>
                <row><cell><p><m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m></p></cell></row>
                <row><cell><p><m>\infty-\infty</m></p></cell></row>
                <row><cell><p><m>0 \cdot \infty</m></p></cell></row>
                <row><cell><p><m>0^0</m></p></cell></row>
                <row><cell><p><m>1^\infty</m></p></cell></row>
                <row><cell><p><m>\infty^0</m></p></cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>All of the expressions above are indeterminate, that is, they do not determine a specific value like 5 or infinity. This section demonstrates techniques to evaluate limits that initially have these forms. The goal is to identify an appropriate method and execute it correctly.</p>

        <theorem xml:id="theorem-lhopital-rule">
            <title>L'Hôpital's Rule</title>
            <statement><p>If <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x)=0</m> and <m>\lim_{x \to a} g(x)=0</m> and <m>\lim_{x \to a} f^\prime(x)</m> and <m>\lim_{x \to a} g^\prime(x)</m> exist then <md>\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \lim_{x \to a} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}.</md></p></statement>
        </theorem>

        <p>The theorem is also true when both numerator and denominator are infinite.</p>

        <example xml:id="example-lhopital-rule-00">
            <title>Example of Using L'Hôpital's Rule</title>
            <statement><p>Evaluate <m>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{e^x-1}{x}</m>.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><md>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{e^{ax}-1}{x} = \amp \amp\amp \text{ attempt to evaluate, and recognize form } 0/0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{ae^{ax}}{1} = \amp \amp \amp \text{ use L'Hôpital's Rule} </mrow>
                    <mrow> a. \amp \amp \amp \text{ this is continuous, so we evaluate.}</mrow>
                </md></p>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection-indeterminate-more">
        <title>More Indeterminate Forms</title>

        <example xml:id="example-zero-infinity">
            <title>Example of <m>0 \cdot \infty</m></title>
            <statement><p>Evaluate <m>\lim_{x \to \pi/2^+} \left(x-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\tan x</m>.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><md>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \pi/2^+} \left(x-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\tan x = \amp\amp\amp \text{ attempt to evaluate, recognize form } 0 \cdot \infty</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \pi/2^+} \frac{x-\frac{\pi}{2}}{\frac{1}{\tan x}} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ clever algebra to convert product to quotient}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \pi/2^+} \frac{x-\frac{\pi}{2}}{\cot x} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ trigonometric identity}</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp\amp\amp \text{ attempt to evaluate, recognize} 0/0</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \pi/2^+} \frac{1}{-\csc^2 x} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ L'Hôpital's Rule} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \pi/2^+} -\sin^2 x = \amp\amp\amp \text{ trigonometric identity}</mrow>
                    <mrow> -1. \amp\amp\amp \text{ this is continuous, so we evaluate} </mrow>
                </md></p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-infinity-infinity">
            <title>Example of <m>\infty-\infty</m></title>
            <statement><p>Evaluate <m>\lim_{\theta \to 0^-} \csc\theta - \cot\theta</m>.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><md>
                    <mrow>\lim_{\theta \to 0^-} \csc \theta - \cot \theta = \amp\amp\amp \text{ attempt to evaluate, recognize form } \infty-\infty</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{\theta \to 0^-} \frac{1}{\sin \theta} - \frac{\cos \theta}{\sin \theta} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ trigonometric identity}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{\theta \to 0^-} \frac{1-\cos \theta}{\sin \theta} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ algebra to convert difference to quotient}</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp\amp\amp \text{ attempt to evaluate, recognize form} 0/0</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{\theta \to 0^-} \frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta} = \amp \amp\amp \text{ L'Hôpital's Rule}</mrow>
                    <mrow> 0 \amp\amp\amp \text{ this is continous, so we evaluate}</mrow>
                </md></p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-indeterminate-exponential">
            <title>Example of an exponential form</title>
            <statement><p>Evaluate <m>\lim_{x \to \infty} \left( 1+\frac{1}{x} \right)^{x^2}</m>.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><md>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} \left( 1+\frac{1}{x} \right)^{x^2} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ recognize form } 1^\infty</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} e^{x^2\ln(1+1/x)} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ definition of } f(x)^{g(x)}</mrow>
                    <mrow>e^{\lim_{x \to \infty} x^2\ln(1+1/x)} \amp\amp\amp \text{ continuity allows substitution}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} x^2\ln(1+1/x) = \amp\amp\amp \text{ attempt to evaluate, recognize form} 0 \cdot \infty</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\ln(1+1/x)}{x^{-2}} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ clever algebra to convert product to quotient}</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp\amp\amp \text{ attempt to evaluate, recognize form} 0/0</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\frac{1}{1+1/x}(-x^{-2})}{-2x^{-3}} = \amp\amp\amp \text{L'Hôpital's Rule} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{x}{2(1+1/x)} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ algebra}. </mrow>
                    <mrow>\infty \amp\amp\amp \text{ evaluate using known form }</mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} \left( 1+\frac{1}{x} \right)^{x^2} = \amp\amp\amp \text{ Return to original limit} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} e^{x^2\ln(1+1/x)} = \amp </mrow>
                    <mrow>\lim_{u \to \infty} e^u = \amp\amp\amp \text{ Use substitution}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\infty \amp\amp\amp \text{ evaluate using known form}</mrow>
                </md></p>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </subsection>

</section>
